diagnosis_text:
No fault found. Router16 is online, forwarding, and participating in OSPF correctly.

Evidence:
- OSPF adjacencies on router16 are Full: to router7 over eth2 (router ID 192.168.10.1, neighbor address 172.30.1.2) and to the campus distribution over bond0 (router ID 172.16.30.254, neighbor address 10.2.2.2). “show ip ospf neighbor” on both router16 and router7 confirms Full state on these links.
- Router16’s interfaces are up where expected: eth2 is UP with 172.30.1.1/24 and bond0 is UP with 10.2.2.1/24.
- Router16’s RIB contains OSPF-learned routes to Campus 1 VLANs 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2, and to the rest of the network via 172.30.1.2.
- End-to-end reachability is intact: multiple hosts in 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 successfully ping hosts in other domains (e.g., 10.6.0.4) with 0% loss.

Mechanism/Localization: If router16 were “down,” we would see OSPF neighbors down and widespread loss to off-subnet destinations. Instead, adjacencies on router16 (eth2 and bond0) and router7 (eth4) are stable and routes are installed.

Root cause: No ongoing network failure; the report likely stemmed from a transient OSPF flap or a monitoring false alarm (neighbor uptimes are ~1 minute, suggesting a recent but recovered event).

Corrective fix: None required. Recommend clearing the alert, reviewing monitoring thresholds and logs on router16 for any brief OSPF restarts, and continuing to observe. 
